\import{fnip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fnip}
\taxon{Definition}
\title{Nullcline}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{Let #{f=(f_{1},f_{2})} be a \vocabk{vector field}{fnip-0009} on an
open set in the plane. The \newvocab{nullcline} of the first component
is the set of points where #{f_{1}} vanishes, and the nullcline of the
second component is the set where #{f_{2}} vanishes. On the first of
these sets the velocity is vertical. On the second it is horizontal.
A point that lies on both is a point where #{f} itself vanishes.}

\p{A point #{x_{*}} at which #{f(x_{*})=0} is an
\newvocab{equilibrium} of #{f}. The constant map #{t\mapsto x_{*}} is
then a \vocabk{trajectory}{fnip-0009} defined for every real time, and
the \vocabk{flow}{fnip-0004} fixes #{x_{*}}. In the plane, the
equilibria are the intersections of the two nullclines.}

\p{\citet{§4.1.1, p. 92}{izhikevich2007dynamical} defines a nullcline
by the vanishing of one component and identifies an intersection of the
nullclines with an equilibrium.
\citet{§3.3, p. 52}{ermentrout2010mathematical} states the same
vanishing condition for the Morris–Lecar components #{\dot V} and
#{\dot n}.
\citet{Definition 1.3, §1.2, p. 9}{kuznetsov2004elements} calls a point
an equilibrium when every evolution operator fixes it.}
